WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.333
STEPHANIE WONG: Machine
learning is everywhere you look,

00:00:02.333 --> 00:00:03.960
and it's made up of
different pieces.

00:00:03.960 --> 00:00:05.280
When building a
model, you may not

00:00:05.280 --> 00:00:07.080
be thinking about the
infrastructure needed

00:00:07.080 --> 00:00:10.150
to train and serve it, and
that's why Kubeflow exists.

00:00:10.150 --> 00:00:12.210
Let's talk about what
Kubeflow is trying to solve

00:00:12.210 --> 00:00:13.320
and how you can use it.

00:00:13.320 --> 00:00:15.690
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:17.590 --> 00:00:19.240
Welcome to Kubeflow 101.

00:00:19.240 --> 00:00:21.423
Today, we'll be giving an
introduction to Kubeflow.

00:00:21.423 --> 00:00:23.590
If you want to check out
the documentation directly,

00:00:23.590 --> 00:00:26.110
check out the link below to
read more about Kubeflow.

00:00:26.110 --> 00:00:28.300
Now, when it comes to
maintaining machine learning

00:00:28.300 --> 00:00:31.240
systems, it can be especially
challenging to manage

00:00:31.240 --> 00:00:34.930
all the application, platform,
and resource considerations.

00:00:34.930 --> 00:00:36.460
ML apps have a
different footprint

00:00:36.460 --> 00:00:38.590
from other web or
mobile deployments.

00:00:38.590 --> 00:00:41.650
For example, a training
phase is resource-intensive,

00:00:41.650 --> 00:00:44.260
while an inference phase
is lightweight and speedy.

00:00:44.260 --> 00:00:46.130
You'll also need the
tools and frameworks

00:00:46.130 --> 00:00:47.770
to run all of these pieces.

00:00:47.770 --> 00:00:50.260
Existing solutions that are
great for building applications

00:00:50.260 --> 00:00:54.010
in other domains don't capture
the nuances needed for ML.

00:00:54.010 --> 00:00:56.500
So Kubeflow is all about
creating a standard

00:00:56.500 --> 00:00:59.650
for deploying enterprise ML
apps, not just development

00:00:59.650 --> 00:01:02.380
or production, but
the entire lifecycle.

00:01:02.380 --> 00:01:04.060
Kubeflow is an
open-source project

00:01:04.060 --> 00:01:06.610
that contains a curated
set of compatible tools

00:01:06.610 --> 00:01:09.100
and frameworks specific for ML.

00:01:09.100 --> 00:01:10.750
One of the primary
goals for Kubeflow

00:01:10.750 --> 00:01:14.440
is to make it easy for everyone
to develop, deploy, and manage

00:01:14.440 --> 00:01:16.720
portable, scalable
machine learning.

00:01:16.720 --> 00:01:18.220
It may not surprise
you to hear it's

00:01:18.220 --> 00:01:21.220
built on top of Kubernetes,
an open-source platform

00:01:21.220 --> 00:01:23.590
for running and
orchestrating containers.

00:01:23.590 --> 00:01:25.600
Kubernetes is built
to run consistently

00:01:25.600 --> 00:01:28.090
across different
environments, and this is

00:01:28.090 --> 00:01:29.890
key to how Kubeflow operates.

00:01:29.890 --> 00:01:31.330
Let's talk about
three principles

00:01:31.330 --> 00:01:34.840
that Kubeflow is built around,
composability, portability,

00:01:34.840 --> 00:01:35.860
and scalability.

00:01:35.860 --> 00:01:38.710
Composability means that you
can choose what's right for you

00:01:38.710 --> 00:01:40.060
on your project.

00:01:40.060 --> 00:01:41.710
People starting out
in machine learning

00:01:41.710 --> 00:01:44.510
tend to be focused on the
end result of the model,

00:01:44.510 --> 00:01:46.990
but there's more than
just building a model.

00:01:46.990 --> 00:01:50.660
The process that goes into an
ML project has many stages,

00:01:50.660 --> 00:01:53.080
and each stage is made up
of different building blocks

00:01:53.080 --> 00:01:54.310
that you choose.

00:01:54.310 --> 00:01:56.530
It shouldn't matter if you
have special requirements

00:01:56.530 --> 00:01:58.750
for your ML
workflow, like if you

00:01:58.750 --> 00:02:00.910
need to use different
versions of TensorFlow

00:02:00.910 --> 00:02:02.540
for different parts
of your project.

00:02:02.540 --> 00:02:04.750
Kubeflow extends
Kubernetes' ability

00:02:04.750 --> 00:02:07.390
to run independent
and configurable steps

00:02:07.390 --> 00:02:11.030
with machine-learning-specific
frameworks and libraries.

00:02:11.030 --> 00:02:14.000
Portability means you can
run all the pieces of your ML

00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:16.370
project anywhere you're
running Kubeflow.

00:02:16.370 --> 00:02:18.560
So it handles the
platform specifics,

00:02:18.560 --> 00:02:20.660
and you can just
focus on your model.

00:02:20.660 --> 00:02:23.790
Write your code once, and let
Kubeflow handle the abstraction

00:02:23.790 --> 00:02:26.090
so you can run it on your
laptop, an on-prem training

00:02:26.090 --> 00:02:27.740
rig, and in the cloud.

00:02:27.740 --> 00:02:29.630
Scalability means
your projects can

00:02:29.630 --> 00:02:31.850
access more resources
when they're needed

00:02:31.850 --> 00:02:33.560
and release them
when they're not.

00:02:33.560 --> 00:02:36.110
Every environment can have
different computing resources

00:02:36.110 --> 00:02:39.380
like CPUs, GPUs, and
TPUs, but you may also

00:02:39.380 --> 00:02:42.020
need to scale across teams
and running experiments.

00:02:42.020 --> 00:02:45.050
Kubeflow leverages the
abilities built into Kubernetes

00:02:45.050 --> 00:02:47.480
to maximize available
resources and scale

00:02:47.480 --> 00:02:49.650
with as little manual
effort as possible.

00:02:49.650 --> 00:02:51.500
So if you're familiar
with Kubernetes,

00:02:51.500 --> 00:02:53.180
you can probably
see how it enables

00:02:53.180 --> 00:02:55.050
Kubeflow to do all of these.

00:02:55.050 --> 00:02:56.660
But there's also a
lot of complexity

00:02:56.660 --> 00:02:59.870
in knowing how Kubernetes works,
which can require expertise

00:02:59.870 --> 00:03:01.860
in many additional concepts.

00:03:01.860 --> 00:03:04.490
Another option may be
hosted ML offerings,

00:03:04.490 --> 00:03:06.800
but that may not work if
you have on-prem hardware

00:03:06.800 --> 00:03:08.090
requirements.

00:03:08.090 --> 00:03:11.060
Kubeflow is designed to let
Kubernetes do what it's good at

00:03:11.060 --> 00:03:13.190
and allow you to focus
on defining and running

00:03:13.190 --> 00:03:15.590
ML workflows, including
managing data,

00:03:15.590 --> 00:03:18.680
running notebooks, training
models, and serving them.

00:03:18.680 --> 00:03:21.870
It's also open-source and
has a growing community.

00:03:21.870 --> 00:03:24.750
Now, that's a pretty high-level
overview of what Kubeflow is.

00:03:24.750 --> 00:03:26.720
In the next video, we'll
go through setting it

00:03:26.720 --> 00:03:28.557
up and running an example.

00:03:28.557 --> 00:03:30.890
But you can also try it out
for yourself on Google Cloud

00:03:30.890 --> 00:03:32.950
by checking out the
link below and visiting

00:03:32.950 --> 00:03:34.700
kubeflow.org to read more.

00:03:34.700 --> 00:03:37.450
[MUSIC PLAYING]

